    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d462">

                <pb facs="0532" n="520" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg520"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.76 Monitoring/576</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Reed</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 9269</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1943-03-06"
                            >March 6, 1943</date>.<lb/>[Received March 13.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to my telegram
                    no. 503 of March 4, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="38" xml:id="d462-f1"/> in which it was
                    stated that the Argentine authorities could <pb facs="0533" n="521"
                        xml:id="pg521"/> not permit the “intervention” of our Military Attaché in
                    connection with the clandestine radio program. Further inquiry on this point
                    elicited an informal undated memorandum (received on March 5) from an official
                    of the Foreign Office, a copy and translation of which are forwarded
                        herewith.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d462-f2"/></p>
                <p>From this it is indicated that not only will the Military Attaché be excluded
                    from active participation but that it might be awkward to deliver the
                        equipment<anchor n="40" xml:id="d462-f3"/> to the Argentine authorities on a
                    loan basis only. This latter subject was discussed in detail in the Embassy’s
                    strictly confidential despatch no. 9124 of February 27.<anchor n="39"
                        xml:id="d462-f4"/></p>
                <p>As soon as a definite reply is received concerning the disposition of the
                    equipment now in possession of the Military Attaché, an informal discussion will
                    be had with officials of the Posts &amp; Telegraphs Department to work out a
                    system of collaboration. However, as stated in the last paragraph of the
                    Embassy’s strictly confidential despatch no. 9237 of March 4,<anchor n="39"
                        xml:id="d462-f5"/> it is rumored that changes may be effected in the
                    administration of the radio-communication section of the Posts &amp; Telegraphs
                    Department, and this might delay a satisfactory solution of the matter.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward L. Reed</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
